### Describe the bug

From QA [thread](https://github.com/storybookjs/storybook/discussions/25690?sort=new#discussioncomment-8278153)

**Project** Storyblok
**Version** 8.0.0-alpha.15

I added the following story:

```js
import { userEvent, within, expect } from '@storybook/test'
import SbButton from './SbButton.vue'

export default {
  component: SbButton,
  argTypes: {
    handleClick: { type: 'function' },
  },
  parameters: {
    actions: { argTypesRegex: '^handle[A-Z].*' },
  },
}

export const Default = {
  args: {
    label: 'Hmmhahahah',
    size: 'large',
  },
}

export const Click = {
  ...Default,
  play: async (context: any): Promise<void> => {
    const { canvasElement } = context
    const canvas = within(canvasElement)
    await userEvent.click(canvas.getByRole('button'))
    expect(context.args.handleClick).toHaveBeenCalled()
  },
}

export const ClickMocked = {
  ...Click,
  args: {
    ...Click.args,
    handleClick: fn(),
  },
}
```

It gives the following error in both the UI and the browser console, which seems incorrect:

<img width="782" alt="image" src="https://github.com/storybookjs/storybook/assets/488689/f2ff013e-ab38-47bf-8ee2-83f52d3bb8ca">


### To Reproduce

_No response_

### System

_No response_

### Additional context

_No response_

- ~Requires https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/105192 (for refactor reasons).~
- Follow-up: https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/106016

This is a first step towards adding first-class types to Godot.
This is better explained in the proposal: https://github.com/godotengine/godot-proposals/issues/12323.

After this PR, we can slowly build on top of it to move things from `ClassDB` into `GDType`.

## Implementation

I piggy-back off the previous `get_class_namev` scaffolding, using the same mechanism to use and assign `GDType *` instead. For this reason, the implementation is safe and battle tested before being implemented.

In particular, each `Object` owns a `const GDType *` to its own type. The type exposes methods for getting type-related information.

I make use of this new construct by optimizing `is_class`. Currently, it is implemented as a virtual function, which uses `GDCLASS` to check against static `StringName` singletons:

https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/e37c6261ea48b1a339c469282df7e9b7c073a72f/core/object/object.h#L432-L437

This is slow, not just because various checks were repeatedly called for each subclass, but also because the elements weren't local in memory, and each had to be put into cache separately.
Instead, I create a constant `LocalVector` of the hierarchy for each type, and simply loop through it to find the queried class name.
(This would be _much_ faster if the argument was a `StringName` instead of `String`, but that will happen sooner or later anyway as the code is refactored and optimized)

## Benchmark
I benchmarked a ~3x improvement of `is_class`. The improvement would likely be better outside benchmarks because there are fewer cache misses.

```c++
	// Setup
	Node3D *node = memnew(Node3D);
	String class_name = "Node2D";

	auto t0 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
	for (int i = 0; i < 500000000; i ++) {
		// Test
		node->is_class(class_name);
	}
	auto t1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
	std::cout << std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::milliseconds>(t1 - t0).count() << "ms\n";
```

This printed:
- `7845ms` on master
- `2662ms` on this PR

- ~Requires https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/105192 (for refactor reasons).~
- Follow-up: https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/106016

This is a first step towards adding first-class types to Godot.
This is better explained in the proposal: https://github.com/godotengine/godot-proposals/issues/12323.

After this PR, we can slowly build on top of it to move things from `ClassDB` into `GDType`.

## Implementation

I piggy-back off the previous `get_class_namev` scaffolding, using the same mechanism to use and assign `GDType *` instead. For this reason, the implementation is safe and battle tested before being implemented.

In particular, each `Object` owns a `const GDType *` to its own type. The type exposes methods for getting type-related information.

I make use of this new construct by optimizing `is_class`. Currently, it is implemented as a virtual function, which uses `GDCLASS` to check against static `StringName` singletons:

https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/e37c6261ea48b1a339c469282df7e9b7c073a72f/core/object/object.h#L432-L437

This is slow, not just because various checks were repeatedly called for each subclass, but also because the elements weren't local in memory, and each had to be put into cache separately.
Instead, I create a constant `LocalVector` of the hierarchy for each type, and simply loop through it to find the queried class name.
(This would be _much_ faster if the argument was a `StringName` instead of `String`, but that will happen sooner or later anyway as the code is refactored and optimized)

## Benchmark
I benchmarked a ~3x improvement of `is_class`. The improvement would likely be better outside benchmarks because there are fewer cache misses.

```c++
	// Setup
	Node3D *node = memnew(Node3D);
	String class_name = "Node2D";

	auto t0 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
	for (int i = 0; i < 500000000; i ++) {
		// Test
		node->is_class(class_name);
	}
	auto t1 = std::chrono::high_resolution_clock::now();
	std::cout << std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::milliseconds>(t1 - t0).count() << "ms\n";
```

This printed:
- `7845ms` on master
- `2662ms` on this PR

### Describe the project you are working on

Godot.

### Describe the problem or limitation you are having in your project

### Abstract

Godot currently does not have a formal type system for its `Object` types. This leads to inefficiencies and difficult to understand code. I propose a stable ABI for `Object` structure data, such that code can be simplified and be made faster.

This is a rough _long term_ proposal. It is not possible to implement it in an afternoon. It exists mostly to be subject to discussion!

### Quick background about types

A type (or "class"), in programming, is used to define and understand objects (formally: structured slices of data). Technically, "types" refers to two separate concepts:

- **Static types**, knowledge known at compile time (to both programmer and compiler). Can be used to protect the programmer and accelerate calls and data access.
- **Runtime types**, knowledge known only at runtime (of any subtype of the static type).

To select functions at runtime, many languages employ virtual tables ("vtables"): Essentially, it means storing a function pointer in the object for every virtual function the object declares. When the virtual function should be called, the corresponding vtable entry is used, and a virtual call to the stored address is made.

### `Object` types

`Object` uses static types sparingly: 
- Engine code uses `C++` types to select functions, but this is not known to the exposed typing system.
- GDExtensions can use static types supported by the respective language. Additionally, non-virtual method calls are accelerated by caching the function pointer by querying Godot.
- GDScript does not use static types to select functions. Effectively, all methods are virtual functions, and there is no function overloading.

Godot has a much more complex relationship to runtime types: In order, first comes `C++` inheritance, then `GDExtension` inheritance, and then `scripting` inheritance. Here's an example:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Object -->|C++ subclass| Node
    Node -->|GDExtension subclass| NodeExtension
    NodeExtension -->|Script subclass| NodeScript
    NodeScript
```

`C++` types (as used in the engine code) cannot be changed after an `Object`'s construction. I will call these *hard* types for the remainder of the article.
`GDExtension` subtypes are defined through pointers. They could *technically* could be changed after construction, although they are not expected to do so. I will consider these to be *hard*, because that's how I see the intention of their design.
`Script` subtypes are supported and expected to change at any time. I will refer to changing types as *soft* types. Note that `Script` subtypes might be removed in the future (no proposal is up for this yet, but it's been discussed).

The fact that part of an `Object`'s type can change at any time is interesting, and (as far as I'm aware) pretty unique in the programming world.

### GDExtension call types

Godot has [3 GDExtension APIs](https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/scripting/gdextension) for object calls (in order of efficiency):
- `ptrcall`: Calls where the arguments and return types are passed with known binary layout (analogous to a regular C call).
- `validated_call`: Calls where arguments are passed as `Variant`, but the types are known to match the arguments' types.
- `call`: Calls where arguments are passed as `Variant`, and the types are dynamic and may not match the expected arguments' types.

All 3 call types are important for Godot, and should be respected for a stable ABI.


### Describe the feature / enhancement and how it helps to overcome the problem or limitation

### ABI for data structure

I propose the following ABI for `Object` structure data (example):

```
SomeGDExtensionSubtype2 {
 shared_ptr<ObjectVTable> vtable;
 shared_ptr<HashMap<StringName, void *>> call_method_table;
 ScriptInstance *script_instance;
 [Object payload]
 [Node payload]
 [SomeGDExtensionSubtype1 payload]
 [SomeGDExtensionSubtype2 payload]
}
ScriptInstance {
 [ScriptType1 payload]
 [ScriptType2 payload]
}
ObjectVTable {
 [Object ptrcall_vtable]
 [Object validated_call_vtable]
 [Node ptrcall_vtable]
 [Node validated_call_vtable]
 [SomeGDExtensionSubtype1 ptrcall_vtable]
 [SomeGDExtensionSubtype1 validated_call_vtable]
 [SomeGDExtensionSubtype2 ptrcall_vtable]
 [SomeGDExtensionSubtype2 validated_call_vtable]
 [ScriptType1 ptrcall_vtable]
 [ScriptType1 validated_call_vtable]
 [ScriptType2 ptrcall_vtable]
 [ScriptType2 validated_call_vtable]
}
```

To describe it in words: The *hard* types are part of the same contiguous data block (including GDExtension types). For each subtype, a payload is stored. This allows for pointers to the data block to efficiently use it, even if the data block is actually a subtype of the statically known type.
`ptrcall` and `validated_call` function pointers are stored in shared vtables (`vtable`). These are essentially `void *[]` arrays, and their layout is exposed to GDExtension. In particular, it is exposed that they are linear and contiguous, and the positions of individual pointers within the block has to be queried from Godot (for GDExtensions).

You may have noticed that this mostly matches structural data of simple types in C++. This is no coincidence: It is an excellent model that allows for many simple and efficient assumptions.

In addition, a pointer to another contiguous data block exists, for the script instance. It is structured the same way. There is also a pointer to the *soft* type (script type).
Finally, the object has a `method_table`. `method_table` is not exposed through the ABI (calling a vfunction is instead achieved through a shared static function, by passing the name). The tables are provided by the script's type (and does not have to be allocated anew for each new object of this type).

### Cross-GDExtension inheritance

It will be possible to subclass `Object` types declared by other `GDExtensions` without linking to them. This is because there is no `GDExtensionInstance` pointer, but instead, only parts of the `Object` blob are owned by the `GDExtension`.

It will also be possible for scripts to subtype scripts of other languages (e.g. `C#` from `GDScript` and vice versa).

### ABI for virtual calls

First off, there is one concession that that this proposal makes: The function signature of subclasses overriding methods from their superclasses must match. This requirement is not present in the current definition of `GDScript`.
This requirement is necessary for optionally typed languages to accelerate typed calls (and honors [Liskov substituation principle](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liskov_substitution_principle)). I will now explain why.

First, let's talk `ptrcall` and `validated_call`. They fulfill the same use-case: They are the fastest way to call a function, if you can statically assert you have the right argument count and types. `ptrcall` is meant for low level languages, and `validated_call` for higher level languages without the ability to generate C like code. It is important to make calls to these very fast, because call overhead may otherwise dominate the language's operations and substantially affect performance. If subclasses were allowed to violate the established binary interface, it is not possible to use `ptrcall` unless the runtime type is known. Therefore, all `ptrcall` and `validated_call` vtable entries must guarantee binary type compatibility.
To guarantee compatibility, Godot (or any GDExtension declaring `ptrcall` virtual methods) must offer multiple `ptrcall` and `validated_call` system level functions that are insertable into the slots:
- The default function to call if not overridden (inserted by default).
- A `ptrcall` function that simply calls the respective `validated_call` function (inserted if a high level scripting language overrides the function).
- If interfaces change, `ptrcall` and `validated_call` functions that take in the old interface arguments and call an override using the new interface, and functions that take in the new interface arguments and call an override using the old interface.

Then, there's `call` type calls: If a type has no annotations in a dynamic type scripting language, every call will have to be validated at *runtime*. To generate a call, a compiler will store the function's `StringName` in the virtual machine code. At runtime, it will look up the corresponding entry in `call_method_table`, and call it with the given arguments.

### Describe how your proposal will work, with code, pseudo-code, mock-ups, and/or diagrams

This ABI would require a lot of changed code, but can generally keep binary compatibility and is backwards compatible with older GDExtension versions. I think it will be possible to work towards the change slowly, but at some point, a "big switch" would have to happen, which would likely be a complicated PR.

The described ABI is simple in principle, so I will add a rough implementation example for code within the engine here. Please appreciate that a real implementation would be a lot more complicated.

**GDCLASS**
```c++
class Node: public Object {
	GDCLASS(Node, Object);

protected:
	// Virtual functions.
	GDVIRTUAL_METHOD(void, add_child_notify, Node *p_child);

	// Override.
	void _init();
	String _to_string();
public:
	// Normal public function.
	void set_name(String p_name);
}

void _bind_methods() {
	// Note the complete lack of ClassDB; all binds are handled through the types.
	override_method(init);
	override_method(to_string);

	bind_method(D_METHOD("set_name"), &Node::set_name);

	bind_virtual_method(D_METHOD("add_child_notify"), &Node::add_child_notify);
}

// This will be expanded to

class Node: public Object {
	GDCLASS(Node, Object);

	// VTable.
protected:
	void _add_child_notify(Node *p_child);
	static void _add_child_notify_vtable_entry_idx; // TODO
public:
	_FORCE_INLINE_ void add_child_notify(Node *p_child) {
		ptrcall_vtable[_add_child_notify_vtable_entry_idx](p_child);
	}
protected:

	// Override.
	String _to_string();
public:
	// Normal public function.
	void set_name(String p_name);
}

void _bind_methods() {
	get_static_type().override_method("to_string", &Node::_to_string);

	get_static_type().bind_method(D_METHOD("set_name"), &Node::set_name);

	get_static_type().bind_virtual_method(D_METHOD("add_child_notify"), &Node::add_child_notify, &Node::_add_child_notify, Node::_add_child_notify_vtable_entry);
}
```

You can see that static calls look normal from `c++`. The call syntax is unaffected by whether it is a static or virtual call:
```c++
void test(Node *p_node, Node *p_child) {
	p_node->add_child_notify(p_child);
}
```

A virtual `ptrcall` (from GDExtension) may look like this (after optimization by the compiler):
```c++
void test(NodeSubtype *p_node, Node *p_child) {
	// Note that NodeSubtype * is a pointer to the NodeSubtype struct within the larger Object struct.
	// _add_child_notify_vtable_offset is determined by querying Godot at launch.
	void *vtable_ptr = p_node->ptrcall_vtable + ObjectSubtype::_add_child_notify_vtable_offset;
	using function_type = ...;
	((function_type)(vtable_ptr))(p_node, p_child);
}
```

A virtual `validated_call` (from scripting) may look like this (after optimization by the compiler):
```c++
void test(const Variant &p_node, const Variant &p_child) {
	// _add_child_notify_validated_vtable_offset is determined by querying Godot at launch.
	void *function_ptr = ((Object *)p_node)->validated_call_vtable[ObjectSubtype::_add_child_notify_validated_vtable_offset];

	Variant *args[2] = { &p_node, &p_child };
	uint32_t argcount = 2;

	using call_function_type = ...;
	((call_function_type)(function_ptr))(args, argcount);
}
```

Finally, a virtual untyped `call` (from scripting) may look like this (after optimization by the compiler):
```c++
void test(Variant **p_args, uint32_t p_argcount) {
	ERR_FAIL_COND(p_argcount < 1);
	ERR_FAIL_COND(p_argcount.get_type() != Variant::Object);
	void *function_ptr = ((Object *)p_args[0])->call_method_table["add_child_notify"];

	using call_function_type = ...;
	((call_function_type)(function_ptr))(function_ptr, p_argcount);
}
```


### If this enhancement will not be used often, can it be worked around with a few lines of script?

Nope.

### Is there a reason why this should be core and not an add-on in the asset library?

It's core.

